<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nordalbingia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nordalbingia was the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> north of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, in present-day Holstein and the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. It held out longest against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who in 804 deported its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> and gave much of the land to his Slav allies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a>. Later resettled by Saxons, it formed the basis of the counties of Holstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nordalbingia.jpg" alt="Nordalbingia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nordalbingia_locator_map_(1000).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nordalbingia, in German Nordelbingen, takes its name from Alba, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and was one of the four regions of the medieval Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Angria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. It is the earliest known dominion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a>, writing in 1076, it held three tribal districts: Dithmarschen, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast from the mouth of the Elbe to the Eider; Holstein proper, on the Stör, a tributary of the Elbe; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a>, on the north bank of the Elbe.</p><p>Its tribes were allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> of Land Hadeln south of the river. To the east the Limes Saxoniae, an inaccessible stretch between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Kiel Fjord, divided it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagria">Wagria</a>, where the Slavic Obotrites lived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> in 772. According to the Royal Frankish Annals, the Westphalian noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> refused to attend the assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> in 777 and fled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to Nordalbingia, or perhaps on to the court of the Danish king Sigfred. Even after Widukind submitted and was baptised in 785, the Nordalbingian tribes held out until the Battle of Bornhöved in 798, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and their Obotrite allies under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drasco">Drożko</a> defeated them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> lost 4,000 people, and 10,000 Saxon families were deported to other parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><p>Danish invasions followed the handing of the land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-younger">Charles the Younger</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> back across the Eider in 808. The next year the emperor had Esesfeld Castle built near present-day Itzehoe, and the whole region was taken into the Frankish realm. The Danish March, from the Eider to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>, was probably set up against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Gudfred</a>, and in 811 his successor Hemming agreed in the Treaty of Heiligen that the Eider should be the border. Quarrels went on until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> defeated King Gnupa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> in 934.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s death in 814 the Nordalbingian Saxons were pardoned and their land was taken back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> and restored to them. Some sources say the emperor had meant to found a diocese of Nordalbingia under the priest Heridag, a plan dropped at Heridag's death. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> the region was assigned to the dioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> became archbishop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 831.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nordalbingia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its main regions were Westphalia, Engern (the land of the Angrarii on the middle Weser), Eastphalia and <b>Nordalbingia</b>, north of the Elbe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
